["```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <vector>
#include <string>

void insertAfterUnique(std::vector<std::string>& vec, const std::string& target, const std::string& newVal) {
    // Remove any duplicates of the first string argument.
    vec.erase(std::remove(vec.begin(), vec.end(), target), vec.end());

    for (auto it = vec.begin(); it != vec.end(); ++it) {
        if (*it == target) {
            // If the first string argument is found, insert the second string argument after it.
            ++it;
            if (it != vec.end()) {
                vec.insert(it, newVal);
            } else {
                // If the first string argument was at the end of the vector, append the second string argument.
                vec.push_back(newVal);
            }
            return;
        }
    }

    // If the first string argument is not found in the vector, insert it at the end.
    vec.push_back(target);
    vec.push_back(newVal);
}
```

This function uses C++ STL algorithms to manipulate the input `std::vector<std::string>` in-place for performance reasons. This approach ensures that a large number of elements can be efficiently processed without causing memory bottlenecks or other potential issues. The use of `std::remove` and `erase` to remove any duplicate occurrences of the first string argument provides robustness against input with varying sizes and content.

This code, however, does not handle external data sources' formatting or validation checks for input strings as it was not specified in the problem statement. Depending on the specific requirements for these inputs, additional error checking or formatting can be added to ensure that only valid inputs are processed correctly by this function.
