['Here is a solution that you might find useful. 

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Threading;
using System.Xml;
using System.Xml.XPath;
using System.Net;

public class EnvironmentVariableManager
{
    private static readonly object _lock = new object();

    public string CreateDirectoryAndSetEnvironmentVariable(string directoryName, string envVar)
    {
        lock (_lock) // Thread safety for environment variable access.
        {
            var dirPath = Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable(envVar);
            if (dirPath == null) 
            {
                dirPath = Path.Combine(AppContext.BaseDirectory, directoryName);
                Directory.CreateDirectory(dirPath);

                // Set the environment variable to the path of newly created directory.
                Environment.SetEnvironmentVariable(envVar, dirPath);
            }

            return dirPath;
        }        
    }

    public XmlElement ParseXmlAndGetFirstMatchingElement(string xmlDocumentUrl, string xpathQuery) 
    {
        var request = WebRequest.Create(xmlDocumentUrl);
        using (var response = request.GetResponse())
        using (var stream = new StreamReader(response.GetResponseStream()))
        {
            // Load the XML document.
            XmlDocument xmlDoc = new XmlDocument();
            xmlDoc.LoadXml(stream.ReadToEnd());

            var navigator = xmlDoc.CreateNavigator();

            var iterator = navigator.Select(xpathQuery);
            if (!iterator.MoveNext()) 
                throw new Exception("No element found."); // Throw exception if no match is found.

            XmlNodeType type;
            while (type != XmlNodeType.Element) // Skip until we reach an Element node type.
                type = iterator.Current.NodeType;

            var element = (XmlElement)iterator.Current;

            if (iterator.MoveNext()) 
                throw new Exception("Multiple elements found."); // Throw exception if more than one match is found.

            return element;
        }    
    }
}
```
In the code above, we use a lock object to handle concurrent access to environment variables and directories. This ensures that multiple threads can safely access this critical section of our code without causing race conditions or other issues. 

We also make use of the XmlDocument class in conjunction with XPath queries to parse XML documents. The WebRequest and StreamReader classes are used to fetch XML documents from a URL, which we then load into an XmlDocument for parsing using XPath queries.

Finally, we throw exceptions if no element is found or if multiple elements are found with the given name when calling ParseXmlAndGetFirstMatchingElement(). This ensures that only one element is returned and prevents ambiguity in our results. 

Please note, this code doesn't validate XML document against a schema before parsing it. Adding such functionality would complicate the solution significantly as we need to download the schema file before loading the XML document which introduces additional dependencies and IO operations. As per the task requirements we are not considering such scenarios due to potential complexities involved in fetching remote schema files, validating XML documents against them etc.
']

['Here is a simplified Java code snippet for the described task using Apache Commons Codec's MD5 utility method to calculate MD5 hash, and Log4j for logging errors. 

```java
import org.apache.commons.codec.digest.DigestUtils;
import java.io.*;
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.util.Date;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.logging.FileHandler;
import java.util.logging.Logger;
import java.util.logging.SimpleFormatter;

public class HashUtil {
    private static final Logger logger = Logger.getLogger(HashUtil.class.getName());
    private static FileHandler fh;  

    // Consecutive invalid inputs counter
    private int consecutiveInvalidInputs = 0; 

    // Time of last invalid input
    private long lastInvalidInputTime = -1;

    // Cache for metadata from external service
    private HashMap<String, String> cache = new HashMap<>();

    static {
        try{
            fh = new FileHandler("hash_errors.log", true);
            logger.addHandler(fh);
            SimpleFormatter formatter = new SimpleFormatter();  
            fh.setFormatter(formatter); 
        }catch (Exception e){
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }

    public String getMD5Hash(String input) {
        if (input == null || input.isEmpty()) {
            consecutiveInvalidInputs++;
            lastInvalidInputTime = new Date().getTime();

            logger.severe("Invalid input: " + input);

            if (consecutiveInvalidInputs > 3 && 
                new Date().getTime() - lastInvalidInputTime <= 5 * 60 * 1000) {
                // Temporarily disable method for 5 minutes if more than 3 invalid inputs in a row.
                logger.severe("Method disabled due to excessive consecutive invalid inputs");
                return "";
            }

            return "";
        } else {
            try {
                String cachedResult = cache.get(input);

                if (cachedResult != null) {
                    // If the input is in cache, return it directly
                    return cachedResult;
                } else {
                    MessageDigest md5 = DigestUtils.getMd5Digest();
                    byte[] digest = md5.digest(input.getBytes("UTF-8"));

                    // External service call here, assuming it's a blocking operation
                    String metadata = getMetadataFromExternalService(input);

                    // Cache the result from external service
                    cache.put(input, metadata);

                    return bytesToHex(digest);
                }  
            } catch (Exception e) {
                logger.severe("Error calculating MD5 hash: " + e.getMessage());
                return "";
            }
        }
    }

    private String getMetadataFromExternalService(String input) throws InterruptedException{
        // Assume this is a blocking operation and may fail or be slow
        Thread.sleep(1000);

        // Replace with actual call to external service here
        return "metadata";
    }

    private static String bytesToHex(byte[] hash) {
        StringBuffer hexString = new StringBuffer();
        for (int i = 0; i < hash.length; i++) {
            String hex = Integer.toHexString(0xff & hash[i]);
            if(hex.length() == 1) hexString.append('0');
            hexString.append(hex);
        }
        return hexString.toString();
    }
}
```
This code assumes an external service call that may fail or be slow, which is a common scenario in real-world applications. The method `getMetadataFromExternalService` simulates such a case by sleeping for 1 second.

The cache strategy here is very basic: it just caches the result of the metadata from the external service. A more sophisticated caching strategy might include time-based expiration, least recently used (LRU) replacement policy, or both. The choice depends on your specific requirements and constraints, such as memory usage, cache hit rate, etc.
