["Here is a simple implementation of an Octree data structure in C++. It's important to note that the memory management used here is simplistic for clarity, but in practice you would want to use a more efficient allocation scheme that minimizes fragmentation and avoids unnecessary allocations or deallocations:

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <stdexcept>

class Octree {
public:
    struct Node {
        std::vector<Node*> children;
        std::vector<int> data;  // Example data type is int

        Node() {}
        ~Node() {
            for (Node* child : children) {
                delete child;
            }
        }
    };

    Octree(size_t featureSize, size_t leafNodes, size_t dataCapacity) 
    : root_(new Node()), featureSize_(featureSize), leafNodes_(leafNodes), dataCapacity_(dataCapacity) {
        if (root_ == nullptr) throw std::bad_alloc();
    }

    void addData(int value) {
        if (root_->data.size() >= dataCapacity_) splitNode(root_);

        root_->data.push_back(value);
    }

private:
    Node* root_;
    size_t featureSize_, leafNodes_, dataCapacity_;

    void splitNode(Node* node) {
        if (node->children.empty()) {
            for (size_t i = 0; i < 8; ++i) {
                Node* newChild = new Node();
                if (newChild == nullptr) throw std::bad_alloc();

                node->children.push_back(newChild);
            }
        }

        for (int value : node->data) {
            for (Node* child : node->children) {
                if (child->data.size() < dataCapacity_) {
                    child->data.push_back(value);
                    break;
                }
            }
        }

        node->data.clear();
    }
};
```
The `Octree` class represents an octree with nodes and their corresponding data arrays. The `splitNode()` function is used to handle the case when a node's capacity is exceeded, by splitting it into eight child nodes and redistributing its data among them. 

In a larger system, you would want to separate the memory management code from the Octree class for better scalability and maintainability. You might also need to add methods for querying or removing data from the tree, as well as handling external data sources more robustly.
"]

["Here's a basic implementation in C with comments explaining how it works:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>
#define MAX_BUFFER_SIZE 1024
#define SENSOR_COUNT 5 // Change this according to your sensor count

typedef struct {
    time_t timestamp;
    double value;
} DataPoint;

typedef struct {
    DataPoint buffer[MAX_BUFFER_SIZE];
    int head;
    int tail;
    int size;
} CircularBuffer;

typedef struct {
    double weightedAverage;
    time_t lastUpdatedTime;
} Metric;

Metric metrics[SENSOR_COUNT];
CircularBuffer dataBuffers[SENSOR_COUNT];

void pushDataPoint(int sensorId, DataPoint dp) {
    if (dataBuffers[sensorId].size == MAX_BUFFER_SIZE) {
        // If buffer is full, pop the oldest element
        dataBuffers[sensorId].head = (dataBuffers[sensorId].head + 1) % MAX_BUFFER_SIZE;
    } else {
        dataBuffers[sensorId].size++;
    }

    // Add new data point to buffer
    dataBuffers[sensorId].buffer[dataBuffers[sensorId].tail] = dp;
    dataBuffers[sensorId].tail = (dataBuffers[sensorId].tail + 1) % MAX_BUFFER_SIZE;
}

void updateMetrics(int sensorId, DataPoint dp) {
    Metric *metric = &metrics[sensorId];
    time_t now = time(NULL);

    // Calculate weighted average considering the age of the data point
    double weight = 1.0 - (now - metric->lastUpdatedTime) / 5.0;
    metric->weightedAverage = (metric->weightedAverage * weight + dp.value) / (weight + 1);

    // Update last updated time
    metric->lastUpdatedTime = now;
}

void processDataPoint(int sensorId, DataPoint dp) {
    pushDataPoint(sensorId, dp);
    updateMetrics(sensorId, dp);
}

double getMaxWeightedAverage() {
    double maxWeightedAverage = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i < SENSOR_COUNT; ++i) {
        if (metrics[i].weightedAverage > maxWeightedAverage) {
            maxWeightedAverage = metrics[i].weightedAverage;
        }
    }

    return maxWeightedAverage;
}
```

This code implements a circular buffer for each sensor data stream and an array of Metric objects to store weighted average and last updated time. The `processDataPoint` function is used to handle incoming data, while `getMaxWeightedAverage` can be called periodically to get the maximum weighted average. 

Please note that you need to adjust this code according to your specific needs, for example by adding error checking or handling different types of sensor data. Also, don't forget to manage system dependencies like memory allocation and deallocation in a way that prevents resource leaks. For high-frequency streams, consider using multithreading or other techniques to handle the load more efficiently.
"]

