#include <thread>
```
The C function to allocate memory for and initialize two arrays, one for `long` offset values and one for `int` keys: 

```cpp
std::mt19937 generator;
std::mutex mtx;

struct record{
    int value;
};

void generateRecords(size_t numOfRecords){
    std::set<long> uniqueOffsets;
    static long* offsetArray = nullptr;  //static allocation for offset array

    if (numOfRecords <= 0 || numOfRecords > UINT32_MAX) {
        printf("Invalid number of records.\n");
        return;
    }

    try{
        offsetArray = new long[numOfRecords];  //dynamic allocation for offset array
    }catch(std::bad_alloc& ba){
        printf("Allocation error: %s\n", ba.what());
        delete[] offsetArray;
        return;
    }

    int* keyArray = new (std::nothrow) int[numOfRecords]; //dynamic allocation for key array
    if(!keyArray){
        printf("Allocation error: Memory not allocated.\n");
        delete[] offsetArray;
        return;
    }

    record* recordsArray = new (std::nothrow) record[numOfRecords]; //dynamic allocation for records array
    if(!recordsArray){
        printf("Allocation error: Memory not allocated.\n");
        delete[] offsetArray;
        delete[] keyArray;
        return;
    }

    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx); //thread-safe mechanism to handle concurrent access
    for(size_t i = 0; i < numOfRecords; ++i){
        long offset = generator();  //generate random number

        while(!uniqueOffsets.insert(offset).second) //ensure unique key by checking if insertion was successful 
            offset = generator();

        offsetArray[i] = offset;  //assign the generated random number to the offset array
        keyArray[i] = static_cast<int>(offset); //assign casted value of offset into key array
        recordsArray[i].value = keyArray[i];
    }

    printf("Total memory allocated: %zu bytes\n", (numOfRecords * sizeof(long)) + (numOfRecords * sizeof(int)) + (numOfRecords * sizeof(record)));  //printing total memory allocated

    delete[] offsetArray;
    delete[] keyArray;
    delete[] recordsArray;
}
```
The function `generateRecords` accepts the number of records to initialize and dynamically allocates three arrays: one for `long` offset values, another for `int` keys, and a third for `record` structures. It uses a set to ensure that no two records have the same key value. The thread-safe mechanism is achieved by using a mutex lock guard. After completing all memory allocations, it prints out the total amount of allocated memory. 

It's worth mentioning that this function does not include error checking for the external data source API. If you need to support large numbers of records or balance between performance and complexity, you may want to consider additional factors such as using a more complex data structure like `std::map` instead of `std::set`, thread pool, etc. 

This code is basic and doesn't cover all edge cases but should give you a start for your task. It does not include the C++ namespace/class and integration with external data source part. Also this solution does not handle errors or exceptions during memory allocation to keep it simple. In practice, handling these scenarios would be necessary.
