["Here's a basic JavaScript function which does what you're asking. It uses the `querySelectorAll` method for efficient DOM traversal and selection, and implements lazy loading by limiting the number of elements processed at once. However, please be aware that this code doesn't integrate with existing libraries or frameworks, handle cross-browser compatibility issues gracefully, interact with third-party ad blockers or browser extensions that may modify the DOM dynamically, extend filtering criteria, or handle unexpected changes in the DOM structure due to user interactions or external data sources.

```javascript
function recursiveTraversal(selector, attributes = {}, textContent = '', limit = 100) {
    let matchingElements = [];
    let elementsRemaining = true;
    let index = 0;

    const checkCriteriaMatching = (element) => Object.entries(attributes).every(([key, value]) => 
        element.getAttribute(key) === value
    ) && (textContent ? element.textContent.includes(textContent) : true);

    while (elementsRemaining && index < document.querySelectorAll(selector).length) {
        const batch = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll(selector)).slice(index, index + limit);

        for (let element of batch) {
            if (checkCriteriaMatching(element)) matchingElements.push(element);
        }

        elementsRemaining = document.querySelectorAll(selector).length > index + limit;
        index += limit;
    }

    return matchingElements;
}
```

This code will return a list of the elements that match the given selector and additionally meet the criteria specified in `attributes` (which should be an object with attribute names as keys and their desired values as properties) or text content (if provided). The function limits the number of elements processed at once to improve efficiency, but you can adjust this value according to your needs.
