['Below is a basic C++ skeleton code that outlines how you might implement a custom dynamic memory manager for the `Resource` data structure. This code does not include all the features or optimizations mentioned in the problem description due to its complexity, but it should provide a good starting point for understanding how to manage resources dynamically and efficiently.

```C++
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <ctime>
#include <memory>
#include <mutex>

// Define a resource with an identifier, timestamp and type
struct Resource {
    std::string id;
    time_t timestamp;
    enum Type { TEXT, IMAGE, VIDEO };
    Type type;
};

namespace ResourceManager {

class Manager {
public:
    static std::vector<std::unique_ptr<Resource>> resources;
    static int totalMemoryAllocated;
    static std::mutex mtx;

    static void init(int numResources) {
        if (numResources < 1 || numResources > 1000) {
            throw std::invalid_argument("Number of resources must be between 1 and 1000");
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < numResources; ++i) {
            auto resource = std::make_unique<Resource>();

            // Generate unique id by hashing current time, ensuring uniqueness
            resource->id = std::to_string(std::hash<std::string>{}(std::to_string(clock())));
            resource->timestamp = clock();
            resource->type = Resource::TEXT; // or set any default type

            mtx.lock();
            totalMemoryAllocated += sizeof(*resource);
            resources.push_back(std::move(resource));
            mtx.unlock();
        }
    }

    static void cleanup() {
        // Ensure all unique pointers are destroyed, thus deallocating their resources
        resources.clear();
        totalMemoryAllocated = 0;
    }
};

std::vector<std::unique_ptr<Resource>> Manager::resources = std::vector<std::unique_ptr<Resource>>();
int Manager::totalMemoryAllocated = 0;
std::mutex Manager::mtx = std::mutex();

} // namespace ResourceManager
```
In the above code, `init` function generates a specified number of resources with unique identifiers and timestamps. These resources are stored in a vector of unique pointers (`resources`). The memory size of each resource is accumulated in `totalMemoryAllocated` to track total allocated memory by all functions. Note that this code does not include any mechanism for handling operations on these resources such as create, read or update, as these would require additional logic and could significantly complicate the code. Also, it doesn't integrate with external time sources or components for generating timestamps or other operations gracefully due to its simplicity. It also does not address potential issues with concurrent access to shared resources. 

This solution is a starting point and might need further adjustments based on specific requirements such as the number of operations, resource types, thread safety, memory optimization, external service dependencies etc. This problem requires in-depth understanding of C++ programming and system design principles which can't be fully covered with one code snippet.
