['Here is a simple example of how this could be implemented using C++ with features from C++11 and beyond, including thread safety with `std::mutex`, error handling, and dynamic memory allocation. This does not provide optimal performance for the described constraints but should be a good starting point.

```cpp
#include <unordered_set>
#include <vector>
#include <random>
#include <thread>
#include <mutex>

struct DataPoint {
    int key;
    float value;
    char* str;
};

std::mt19937 gen{std::random_device{}()};
std::uniform_real_distribution<float> floatDist(0.0f, 1.0f);
std::uniform_int_distribution<char> charDist('a', 'z');
std::mutex mtx;
std::unordered_set<int> keys;
size_t allocatedMemory = 0;

DataPoint createDataPoint() {
    DataPoint dp{};
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
    do {
        dp.key = gen();
    } while (keys.find(dp.key) != keys.end());
    keys.insert(dp.key);
    dp.value = floatDist(gen);
    size_t strLen = gen() % 100 + 1;
    dp.str = new char[strLen];
    for (size_t i = 0; i < strLen; ++i) {
        dp.str[i] = charDist(gen);
    }
    allocatedMemory += sizeof(DataPoint) + strLen * sizeof(char);
    return dp;
}

std::vector<DataPoint> initializeRecords(int num_records){
  if (num_records < 1 || num_records > 100'000){
    throw std::invalid_argument("Number of records must be between 1 and 100,000");
  }

  std::vector<DataPoint> data(num_records);
  try {
    for (int i = 0; i < num_records; ++i) {
        data[i] = createDataPoint();
    }
  } catch (const std::bad_alloc& e) {
    // Deallocate memory if allocation fails
    for(auto & dp : data){
      delete[] dp.str;
    }
    throw;
  }

  return data;
}

void deallocateMemory() {
  std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
  for (auto& kv : keys) {
      delete[] kv.str;
  }
  allocatedMemory = 0;
}
```
This code creates a `DataPoint` struct to store key, value and dynamically-allocated string. It generates random key values ensuring they are unique by using an unordered set. It also keeps track of total memory allocated with each allocation for printing at the end. 

It throws an exception if any allocation fails which is then caught in `initializeRecords` function to deallocate all previously allocated memory before rethrowing. Finally, it has a function `deallocateMemory` that can be called externally to free all dynamically-allocated memory when no longer needed. It also uses a mutex for thread safety during dynamic allocation and deallocation of memory.
']

