["Here is a C program that fulfills the described conditions. It uses `<stdlib.h>` for dynamic memory allocation and random number generation functions, `<time.h>` to seed the random number generator with current time, and `<assert.h>` for error checking. 

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <assert.h>

typedef struct {
    double x;
    double y;
} point;

typedef struct {
    int key;
    point* coord;
} record;

void generate_arrays(size_t size, 
                     double min_double, 
                     double max_double, 
                     int min_int, 
                     int max_int) {

    // Allocate memory for arrays
    double* arr_double = malloc(sizeof(*arr_double)*size);
    int* arr_int = malloc(sizeof(*arr_int)*size);
    point* arr_point = malloc(sizeof(*arr_point)*size);
    record* arr_record = malloc(sizeof(*arr_record)*size);

    assert(arr_double != NULL && 
           arr_int != NULL && 
           arr_point != NULL && 
           arr_record != NULL);

    // Seed the random number generator with current time for more randomness
    srand((unsigned int)time(NULL));

    // Initialize arrays with random values within specified ranges
    for (size_t i = 0; i < size; ++i){
        arr_double[i] = min_double + ((double)rand() / RAND_MAX) * (max_double - min_double);
        arr_int[i] = rand() % (max_int - min_int + 1) + min_int;

        // Initialize point coordinates with random values within specified bounds
        arr_point[i].x = min_double + ((double)rand() / RAND_MAX) * (max_double - min_double);
        arr_point[i].y = min_double + ((double)rand() / RAND_MAX) * (max_double - min_double);

        // Set record int field to random value within a given range
        arr_record[i].key = rand() % (max_int - min_int + 1) + min_int;
        arr_record[i].coord = &arr_point[i];  // Set pointer to point in record
    }

    // Use the arrays...

    // Free all allocated memory when no longer needed
    free(arr_double);
    free(arr_int);
    free(arr_point);
    free(arr_record);
}
``` 

The function `generate_arrays` takes as arguments the size of the arrays and the minimum and maximum values for floating-point numbers and integers. This allows the developer to specify the constraints for the data within these types based on requirements or specifications. The memory allocated using malloc is freed when no longer needed to prevent memory leaks. 

