['Here is a basic implementation of the task using `pthread` library for thread safety. It also uses `stdatomic.h` for atomic reference count. Please note that this code does not handle large data sizes or perform extensive performance tests.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <stdatomic.h>

typedef struct Record {
    int key;
    time_t timestamp;
    atomic_int refCount;
} Record;

Record* createDatabase(size_t size) {
    pthread_mutex_t mutex = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

    // Allocate memory for the database
    Record *database = malloc(sizeof(Record)*size);

    if (!database){ 
        fprintf(stderr, "Failed to allocate memory\n");
        exit(-1);
    }

    srand((unsigned int)time(NULL)); // initialize random seed for key generation

    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex); 
    for (size_t i = 0; i < size; ++i){
        database[i].key = rand(); // Generate unique keys
        database[i].timestamp = time(NULL); // Initialize timestamp
        atomic_init(&database[i].refCount, 1); // Initialize ref count to 1
    }
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);

    return database;
}
```
In this code, `pthread_mutex_lock` and `pthread_mutex_unlock` are used for thread safety when accessing the database. The `atomic_init` is used to initialize the reference count of each record atomically. If allocation fails, an error message will be printed to stderr and program will exit with a status code indicating failure.

In a production environment, you would also have functions like `getRecord(int key)`, `acquireReference(Record *record)`, `releaseReference(Record *record)` etc., which are responsible for accessing records by their keys, incrementing and decrementing the reference count respectively. You would also need a function to free memory when records' reference counts reach 0, so that you don't waste memory on unused records.
']

