        }
    }

    srand((unsigned int)time(NULL));

    for (int i = 0; i < size; ++i) {
        offsets[i] = rand();
        keys[i] = rand();
        records[i]->value = rand();

        for (int j = 0; j < 256; ++j) 
            records[i]->str[j] = 'a' + rand() % ('z' - 'a');
    }

    return 0;
}
```

In this implementation, I have used a custom memory allocator (`custom_alloc` and `custom_free`) to manage memory. The function `initialize_data` initializes the dataset with offset values, keys, records, and strings dynamically allocated according to the size parameter. The memory is tracked by storing the pointer to the block of memory in a struct (`custom_mem_block`), which allows for easier management and deallocation later. If any allocation fails or if the input size is out of range, `initialize_data` returns -1.
